    {
      "id": "sess_read_mount_still_takes_a_link",
      "seq": 920040,
      "targets": [
        "ram",
        "disk"
      ],
      "command": "ln -s /ro/y.txt /ro/rolink && readlink /ro/rolink",
      "note": "A read-only MOUNT is a statement about a backend that cannot write, not a ban on names above it: symlinks are namespace state, so a link here needs no write capability from the VFS and the target need not exist. integ/vfs/<svc>/sym.json pins the same rule above postgres, mongodb, chroma and qdrant, all mounted read; those need containers, so /ro guards it on every host. What DOES gate this plane is a session grant -- see sess_read_grant_blocks_link_create.",
      "expect": {
        "exit": 0,
        "stdout": "/ro/y.txt\n",
        "stderr": ""
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sess_read_mount_still_refuses_the_backend_write",
      "seq": 920041,
      "targets": [
        "ram",
        "disk"
      ],
      "command": "echo x > /ro/nope.txt",
      "note": "The other half of the pair: one mount, two planes, opposite answers. A gate that refused the link above would have to refuse this too, and one that allows this would have to allow the link -- so the two cases together are what keep the planes told apart.",
      "expect": {
        "exit": 1,
        "stdout": "",
        "stderr": "/ro/nope.txt: Read-only file system\n"
      }
    },
